/-
Fmt/MustBeRefused/M1ProgPFa.lean: one false proof of the first toy (a product test), as bytes:
cheat-BASE_WRONG_WORD.
This file holds the bytes of that false proof written out as a Lean literal; it was made by a
program and is not meant to be read.

The proof in this file is false and must be refused. A verifier that accepts it is broken. It is
test data of a toy: made-up data only, no model and no weights. It was made by the toy's own prover,
in the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean code), with one block cheating in
the named way and all others going on honestly. These files are test fixtures: proofs that every
verifier must refuse. Test data: every file here is false, or breaks a rule of the protocol, and
must be refused.

The first toy is a test protocol for the claim that three rows a, b, c of one oracle message have
a_i b_i = c_i for every i (the heads of Fmt/M1Types.lean and Fmt/M1Psc.lean). A block is one step of
that protocol. BASE is the last block (the head of Fmt/M1Dec.lean).

Sources. Made by a program from the files as they came. No Mathlib is imported. "cheat-X" is
FALSE-PROOFS-every-verifier-must-refuse/product/full/product.cheat-X.proof of the programs' archive
of files (sha256 f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is
not in this repository): the run at full parameters (the toy at its real target). Their parameters
are those of the honest run (Fmt/M1ProgPFFiles.lean). The false proofs of this run stand one to a
file, to keep the files small; Fmt/MustBeRefused/M1ProgPF.lean gathers them.

1. What the file contains. f_cheat_BASE_WRONG_WORD: the bytes. Which refusal was expected of it is
   recorded in Fmt/MustBeRefused/M1ProgPF.lean.

2. What is checked when the file compiles. Its size, and the format's hash under the number 168
   against a value that the format page's own reference program (format_rules.py) computed from the
   archive's file, at a moment when that file's sha256 agreed with the archive's list of sums.

3. Theorems. No theorem in this file. A test (#guard) is an evaluation made while the file compiles;
   the compile fails if the result moves; it leaves no theorem.

4. What is not claimed. Nothing is said here about what any verifier in Lean answers on it; see
   Fmt/M1FullProgPFc.lean, Run/PlanQSFProgPFe.lean, Run/M1EngineOneF*.lean and
   Run/M1EngineTypedF*.lean.

Notation: `HF`: a hash (Fmt/Rules5Fast.lean); its arguments, in order: number, tree, index, start,
bytes; `zero32`: 32 zero bytes; `hex`: bytes as a string of base 16 (both: Fmt/Rules1.lean).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
